window.OF_SEARCH = [{"title": "Overview", "heading": "", "text": "A file toolkit for Java 17+ with no external libraries : everything is built on the JDK ( java.nio.file , java.net.http , javax.crypto , java.util.zip ). It is the engine of the FileExp file manager and covers what a file-heavy desktop or server application usually has to write by hand: transfers with progress and cancel, a restorable trash, an instant name index, batch rename with preview, duplicate finder, disk analysis, backups and sync, an encrypted vault, WebDAV and SMB storage, and encrypted file sharing on the local network. OctopusFile octo = OctopusFile.of(); octo.write(Path.of(\"notes", "url": "en/docs/index.html"}, {"title": "Overview", "heading": "Feature pages", "text": "[Files](files.md) copy, move, delete, list, read, write OctopusFile , ListOperation , FileEntry [Errors](errors.md) unchecked in the facade, IOException in the modules, machine-readable codes OctopusFileException , FileOperationException , ErrorCodes [Trash](trash.md) restorable trash, the same on every OS TrashEntry , TrashManager [Transfers](transfers.md) queue with progress, speed, ETA, cancel, and pause and resume (2.1) TransferManager , Transfer [Search](search.md) instant name index, search inside files FileIndex , SearchQuery , ContentSearcher [Duplicates and hashing](duplicates.md) dup", "url": "en/docs/index.html#feature-pages"}, {"title": "Overview", "heading": "Public and internal API", "text": "(root) — OctopusFile facade, operations, FileEntry , GlobalConfiguration public modules. — analysis, backup, duplicates, filtering, manipulation, monitoring, organization, reading, search, sync, transfer, trash, utils, writing public infrastructure.compression , .errors , .hashing , .network , .security , .storage public infrastructure.concurrency , .filesystem , .resources internal support. — cache, config, logging, security, validation internal Internal packages and types are marked with [ @Internal ](../../src/com/octopusfile/Internal.java). They are public in Java terms only because other ", "url": "en/docs/index.html#public-and-internal-api"}, {"title": "Overview", "heading": "Renamed in the English API", "text": "The public API used to mix Portuguese and English. If you used the old names: DispositivosExternos ( listarDispositivos , listarRemoviveis , isRemovivel , nomeVolume , tipoSistema ) ExternalDevices ( listDevices , listRemovable , isRemovable , volumeLabel , fileSystemType ) DiscoLocal LocalDiskStorage RedeSMBNFS NetworkShareStorage ArmazenamentoEmNuvem InMemoryBucketStorage OutrosProvedores ( registrar , obter , isRegistrado ) StorageRegistry ( register , get , isRegistered ) StorageProvider : getNome , isDisponivel , abrirLeitura , abrirEscrita , remover , existe getName , isAvailable , openR", "url": "en/docs/index.html#renamed-in-the-english-api"}, {"title": "Overview", "heading": "Design rules", "text": "No external dependencies. A runtime made with jlink needs java.base , java.net.http (WebDAV, downloads), java.xml (WebDAV, Office documents), java.logging and jdk.crypto.ec (sharing nearby). Blocking calls, explicit threads. Methods block; long work goes through TransferManager (progress, cancel) or your own executor. No method needs a UI thread. No silent overwrite of a file. Copying or moving a file refuses an existing target ( FileAlreadyExistsException ); restoring from the trash never replaces anything; received files and TransferManager copies get free names ( photo (2).jpg ). Since 2.0.", "url": "en/docs/index.html#design-rules"}, {"title": "Overview", "heading": "Requirements", "text": "Java 17 or newer. Tested on Windows 11; the code paths for Linux and macOS (mounts, gio , /Volumes ) exist but need more testing.", "url": "en/docs/index.html#requirements"}, {"title": "Files", "heading": "", "text": "OctopusFile is a fluent facade over the everyday file operations. Every method accepts a String or a Path . OctopusFile octo = OctopusFile.of(); octo.write(dir.resolve(\"notes.txt\")).text(\"first line\\n\"); octo.write(dir.resolve(\"notes.txt\")).append(\"second line\\n\"); String text = octo.read(dir.resolve(\"notes.txt\")).asText(); octo.copy(dir.resolve(\"notes.txt\")).to(dir.resolve(\"backup/notes.txt\")); // creates \"backup\" octo.move(dir.resolve(\"backup/notes.txt\")).to(dir.resolve(\"archive/notes-old.txt\"));", "url": "en/docs/files.html"}, {"title": "Files", "heading": "Listing", "text": "list(...) reads the attributes of every item once , together with the directory listing (one system call for the whole folder on Windows), so sorting and filtering never go back to the disk. 50 000 files are listed in about 0.2 s on a warm cache. List<FileEntry> entries = octo.list(dir).includeDirectories().sortByName().entries(); for (FileEntry e : entries) { System.out.println(e.name() + (e.directory() ? \"/\" : \" \" + e.size() + \" bytes\")); } List<Path> texts = octo.list(dir).filter(new TypeFilter(\"txt\")).collect(); includeDirectories() folders too (by default only files) includeHidden(false) ", "url": "en/docs/files.html#listing"}, {"title": "Files", "heading": "Reading and writing", "text": "octo.read(path).forEachLine(System.out::println); // text, line by line List<String[]> rows = octo.read(\"data.csv\").filter(new CsvFilter().withDelimiter(\";\")).collect(); octo.write(\"out.csv\").csv(rows, \";\"); octo.write(\"blob.bin\").bytes(data); For more control use the modules directly: modules.reading ( WholeFileReader , LineReader , CsvReader , BinaryReader ) and modules.writing ( WholeFileWriter , LineWriter , CsvWriter , BinaryWriter ). Before 2.0 WholeFileReader , LineReader , WholeFileWriter and LineWriter were called FileReader , BufferedReader , FileWriter and BufferedWriter , like the ", "url": "en/docs/files.html#reading-and-writing"}, {"title": "Files", "heading": "Deleting", "text": "octo.delete(path) deletes for good. To let the user change their mind, use the [trash](trash.md).", "url": "en/docs/files.html#deleting"}, {"title": "Errors", "heading": "", "text": "Since 2.0 there is one rule for the whole library: The OctopusFile facade and its operations ( read , write , copy …) the unchecked OctopusFileException , with an ErrorCodes value: quick to use, nothing to declare Every module ( modules. , infrastructure. ) java.io.IOException , like java.nio.file.Files : the JDK exception when there is one ( NoSuchFileException , FileAlreadyExistsException , NotDirectoryException , DirectoryNotEmptyException , AccessDeniedException ), otherwise FileOperationException , which keeps the ErrorCodes value Arguments that make no sense (a null , an impossible optio", "url": "en/docs/errors.html"}, {"title": "Trash", "heading": "", "text": "A restorable trash that behaves the same on Windows, Linux and macOS. Each item is compressed into its own ZIP and recorded in an index with its original path, date and sizes. OctopusFile octo = OctopusFile.of(); TrashEntry entry = octo.moveToTrash(file); // compressed, indexed, restorable System.out.println(entry.getOriginalPath() + \" deleted at \" + entry.getDeletedAt()); octo.restoreFromTrash(entry.getId()); // back to the same place listTrash() all TrashEntry items restoreFromTrash(id, otherPath) restore somewhere else (when the original place is taken) purgeFromTrash(id) delete one item fo", "url": "en/docs/trash.html"}, {"title": "Transfers", "heading": "", "text": "TransferManager is a queue (2 workers) for anything that takes time. Each job is a Transfer with bytes and files done, speed (moving average), estimated time left, state and a cancel() method. TransferManager tm = TransferManager.get(); tm.addListener(t -> { if (t != null && !t.isFinished()) { double f = t.getFraction(); // -1 while the total is still unknown String done = f < 0 ? \"...\" : String.format(\"%.0f%%\", f 100); System.out.printf(\"%s %s %,d B/s%n\", t.getTitle(), done, (long) t.getSpeed()); } }); Transfer t = tm.copy(List.of(src), dest, false, null, \"Videos\"); // t.cancel() stops it; wh", "url": "en/docs/transfers.html"}, {"title": "Transfers", "heading": "Built-in jobs", "text": "copy(sources, dir, move, kind, label) local copy or move; free names, never overwrites; getCompleted() lists {source, target} pairs (for undo) upload(files, webDav, remoteDir, label) upload to a [WebDAV](storage.md) cloud download(entries, webDav, localDir, label) download from WebDAV downloadUrl(url, dir, label) download from the internet into a .part file, renamed when complete snapshot(repo, folder, label) a [snapshot backup](backup-sync.md); result: the Snapshot (since 2.0) restore(repo, snapshot, target, only) restore a snapshot into an empty folder (since 2.0) restoreVersion(repo, versio", "url": "en/docs/transfers.html#built-in-jobs"}, {"title": "Transfers", "heading": "Pause and resume (since 2.1)", "text": "t.pause(); // queued: PAUSED at once, takes no worker; running: PAUSED at its next safe point t.resume(); // back to QUEUED (same place in the queue) or RUNNING (from where it stopped) tm.pauseAll(); // every transfer in the list; tm.resumeAll() lifts them all long active = t.getFinishedAt() - t.getStartedAt() - t.getPausedMillis(); A queued transfer that is paused keeps its place, and the next one runs instead. A running one pauses at a safe point (where it also checks for cancel) and keeps its worker and what it has done. isPauseRequested() is true from pause() to resume() ; getState() becom", "url": "en/docs/transfers.html#pause-and-resume-since-21"}, {"title": "Transfers", "heading": "Your own jobs", "text": "Transfer custom = tm.task(Transfer.Kind.COPY, \"Thumbnails\", \"cache\", r -> { r.total(3, 3); // bytes, files (if known) for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) { r.checkCancel(); // throws TransferCancelledException when cancelled r.bytes(1); r.file(\"thumb-\" + i); } }); Kinds: COPY , MOVE , SEND , UPLOAD , DOWNLOAD , BACKUP , SYNC , VAULT , and since 2.0 RESTORE , SEARCH , INDEX , SCAN (only a label for the UI).", "url": "en/docs/transfers.html#your-own-jobs"}, {"title": "Search", "heading": "Instant search by name — FileIndex", "text": "An in-memory index of every name under a folder. Names are stored in columns (no object per file) with an inverted index of trigrams, so a search costs the number of candidates, not the number of files. FileIndex index = new FileIndex(home).build(null, new AtomicBoolean()); SearchResult result = index.search(SearchQuery.of(\"report\").extensions(\"pdf\").limit(50)); for (SearchResult.Hit h : result.hits()) { System.out.println(h.path() + \" \" + h.size()); } of(\"annual report\") every word must appear in the name (case-insensitive); and ? are wildcards extensions(\"pdf\", \"docx\") only these extensions ", "url": "en/docs/search.html#instant-search-by-name-fileindex"}, {"title": "Search", "heading": "Saving the index (since 1.4)", "text": "save(file) writes the index to a compact file and FileIndex.load(file) reads it back much faster than a walk, so an application can search as soon as it starts. build keeps the loaded data searchable until the new index is ready. Path cache = appData.resolve(\"home.index\"); FileIndex index = Files.exists(cache) ? FileIndex.load(cache) // instant : new FileIndex(home).build(null, null); // ... search at once; then, in the background: index.build(null, new AtomicBoolean()); // catch up with the disk index.save(cache); The file holds names, sizes and dates (no content). It is written to a temporar", "url": "en/docs/search.html#saving-the-index-since-14"}, {"title": "Search", "heading": "Search inside files — ContentSearcher", "text": "Finds the text files containing a string, in parallel, reading line by line (files are never fully loaded), UTF-8 first and Latin-1 as fallback, skipping binary files. List<ContentSearcher.Match> matches = new ContentSearcher().maxResults(100) .search(home, \"total: 1.250\", null, new AtomicBoolean()); for (ContentSearcher.Match m : matches) { System.out.println(m.path() + \":\" + m.line() + \" \" + m.excerpt()); } Options: caseSensitive , allMatchesPerFile , maxFileBytes (20 MB by default), extensions(...) (about 40 text and code extensions by default). The onMatch callback receives results as they", "url": "en/docs/search.html#search-inside-files-contentsearcher"}, {"title": "Search", "heading": "Office documents (since 1.4)", "text": "ContentSearcher also looks inside Word, Excel, PowerPoint and LibreOffice files ( docx , xlsx , pptx , odt , ods , odp ); there line() is the number of the paragraph, or of the row in a spreadsheet. DocumentText.lines(file) gives that text directly. Only the JDK is used (the files are ZIP + XML), DTDs and external entities are never read, and at most 200 MB of XML is decompressed per document. Damaged documents are skipped.", "url": "en/docs/search.html#office-documents-since-14"}, {"title": "Duplicates & hashing", "heading": "", "text": "DuplicateFinder compares files by content , in three steps so that most files are never fully read: same size → same hash of the first and last 16 KiB and the size → same full hash (files up to 32 KiB are already read whole by the partial hash, so they skip the last step). List<DuplicateGroup> groups = new DuplicateFinder().minSize(1024 1024).skipHidden(true).find(folder); for (DuplicateGroup g : groups) { System.out.println(g.getFiles().size() + \" copies, \" + g.getWastedBytes() + \" bytes can be freed\"); } find(folder, progress, cancel) reports the file being processed and stops when the Atomi", "url": "en/docs/duplicates.html"}, {"title": "Duplicates & hashing", "heading": "Hashing", "text": "String sha256 = new FileHasher().hash(path); // hex, streaming boolean same = new FileHasher().matches(path, expectedHash); String quick = new FileHasher().partialHash(path, size); // first and last 16 KiB + size new FileHasher(\"SHA-1\") or any other MessageDigest algorithm also works.", "url": "en/docs/duplicates.html#hashing"}, {"title": "Disk analysis", "heading": "", "text": "DiskAnalyzer walks a folder once and returns everything a \"what fills my disk\" screen needs. Only folders are kept in memory (not one object per file), so it handles hundreds of thousands of files. DiskAnalyzer.Result r = new DiskAnalyzer().top(20).analyze(folder, n -> { }, new AtomicBoolean()); System.out.println(r.root.size() + \" bytes in \" + r.root.fileCount() + \" files\"); for (DiskAnalyzer.Folder f : r.root.children()) { // largest first System.out.println(f.name + \" \" + f.size()); } for (DiskAnalyzer.Category c : r.categories) { // images, videos, music, documents, ... System.out.println(", "url": "en/docs/analysis.html"}, {"title": "Disk analysis", "heading": "Treemap layout", "text": "TreemapLayout.squarify turns sizes (largest first) into rectangles with areas proportional to the sizes and shapes close to squares — ready to draw in any UI toolkit. double[] sizes = r.root.children().stream().mapToDouble(DiskAnalyzer.Folder::size).toArray(); List<TreemapLayout.Rect> rects = TreemapLayout.squarify(sizes, 0, 0, 800, 400); // each Rect: x, y, w, h and index (the position in sizes)", "url": "en/docs/analysis.html#treemap-layout"}, {"title": "Batch rename", "heading": "", "text": "BatchRenamer renames many files at once. It works in two steps: a plan (new names and problems, without touching the disk — fast enough to recompute on every key press) and apply (all or nothing). BatchRenamer.Rule rule = new BatchRenamer.Rule(); rule.template = \"Holiday {n}\"; // also {name}, {date}, {ext} rule.digits = 2; BatchRenamer.Plan plan = BatchRenamer.plan(photos, rule, null); for (BatchRenamer.Entry e : plan.entries) { System.out.println(e.source.getFileName() + \" -> \" + e.newName + (e.problem == null ? \"\" : \" (\" + e.problem + \")\")); } if (!plan.hasProblems()) { BatchRenamer.renameAl", "url": "en/docs/rename.html"}, {"title": "Batch rename", "heading": "Rule", "text": "Applied in this order, by default to the name without its extension: template replaces the name: {name} original name, {n} counter ( start , zero-padded to digits ), {date} modified date yyyy-MM-dd , {ext} find , replace , regex , matchCase find and replace, plain text or regular expression ( $1 groups) prefix , suffix add text letterCase KEEP , LOWER , UPPER , TITLE includeExtension apply the rules to the extension too", "url": "en/docs/rename.html#rule"}, {"title": "Batch rename", "heading": "Problems found by the plan", "text": "Duplicate name (two files would get the same name), Already exists (a file not being renamed has that name), Invalid characters , Empty name , Invalid name ( . and .. ), Name too long , Invalid regular expression , plus your own check: the third argument of plan is a Function<String, String> returning an error or null (for example the rules of your operating system).", "url": "en/docs/rename.html#problems-found-by-the-plan"}, {"title": "Batch rename", "heading": "Apply", "text": "renameAll(pairs) uses temporary names only where needed (swaps like a → b and b → a, letter-case changes on Windows/macOS) and undoes every rename if one fails. It returns the pairs applied, which can be passed back reversed to undo. For big selections, read the attributes once with BatchRenamer.prepare(files) and call plan(prepared, rule, check) on every change: 5 000 names in about 0.1 s.", "url": "en/docs/rename.html#apply"}, {"title": "Compression", "heading": "", "text": "OctopusFile.of().compress(folder.resolve(\"Project\"), zip); for (ZipCompressionEngine.Entry e : ZipCompressionEngine.list(zip)) { // central directory only, no extraction System.out.println(e.name() + \" \" + e.size()); } OctopusFile.of().extract(zip, folder.resolve(\"restored\")); ZipCompressionEngine streams with 64 KB buffers and: stores already-compressed formats (jpg, mp4, zip, docx, pdf, …) without compressing them again — same size, much less CPU; keeps modification dates; blocks zip slip : an entry like ../../evil is rejected instead of being written outside the destination; compressAll(sou", "url": "en/docs/compression.html"}, {"title": "Backup & sync", "heading": "", "text": "OctopusFile octo = OctopusFile.of(); BackupMetadata zip = octo.backup(docs).to(usb.resolve(\"zips\")); // Documents-<date>.zip SyncResult copy = octo.sync(docs, usb.resolve(\"Documents\"), false); // only new and changed SyncResult mirror = octo.sync(docs, usb.resolve(\"Documents\"), true); // also removes what was deleted", "url": "en/docs/backup-sync.html"}, {"title": "Backup & sync", "heading": "Backups — BackupService", "text": "A ZIP of a file or folder named with the original name and a timestamp, so a backup never replaces another. backup(path).restoreTo(dir) extracts it (files with the same name in dir are replaced). BackupScheduler runs backups at a fixed rate. Default folder (since 2.1). backup(path).toDefault() saves the backup in the folder of the configuration, GlobalConfiguration.getBackupDirectory() (by default ~/.octopusfile/backup , created when needed). The folder is read at the moment of the call, so a later setBackupDirectory(...) is used by the next backup: octo.getGlobalConfiguration().setBackupDirec", "url": "en/docs/backup-sync.html#backups-backupservice"}, {"title": "Backup & sync", "heading": "Snapshots — SnapshotRepository (since 1.4)", "text": "Incremental, deduplicated and optionally encrypted backups. Each backup records the whole folder as a snapshot , but only content the repository does not have yet is stored. Since 2.0.3 the operations on one open repository run one at a time (a backup and a delete from another thread wait for each other), so a progress callback must not wait for another thread that uses the same repository: try (SnapshotRepository repo = SnapshotRepository.create(usb.resolve(\"Backups\"), \"a long password\".toCharArray())) { SnapshotRepository.Snapshot s = repo.backup(docs, \"Documents\", null, null); System.out.pr", "url": "en/docs/backup-sync.html#snapshots-snapshotrepository-since-14"}, {"title": "Backup & sync", "heading": "Versions of one file (since 2.1)", "text": "For a \"Previous versions\" panel: versions(label, path) lists the different contents one file had in the snapshots of a series, newest first, and restoreVersion brings one of them back to a new file, without restoring the whole snapshot: List<SnapshotRepository.Version> versions = repo.versions(\"Documents\", \"notes/todo.txt\"); for (SnapshotRepository.Version v : versions) { System.out.println(v.snapshot().id() + \" \" + v.size() + \" B \" + Instant.ofEpochMilli(v.modified())); } repo.restoreVersion(versions.get(1), desktop.resolve(\"todo (yesterday).txt\"), null, null); Series the label given to backu", "url": "en/docs/backup-sync.html#versions-of-one-file-since-21"}, {"title": "Backup & sync", "heading": "In the cloud — push and pull (since 2.0)", "text": "A repository can be copied to a WebDAV folder (Nextcloud, ownCloud, a NAS…) and brought back: WebDavStorage cloud = new WebDavStorage(\"https://cloud.example.com/remote.php/dav/files/ana/\", \"ana\", appPassword); SnapshotRepository.SyncReport sent = repo.push(cloud, \"/Backups/Laptop\", null, null); // only what is missing // on another computer, or after losing the disk: SnapshotRepository.pull(cloud, \"/Backups/Laptop\", newFolder, null, null); try (SnapshotRepository copy = SnapshotRepository.open(newFolder, password)) { copy.restore(copy.list().get(copy.list().size() - 1), restoreFolder, null, nu", "url": "en/docs/backup-sync.html#in-the-cloud-push-and-pull-since-20"}, {"title": "Backup & sync", "heading": "Sync — DirectorySynchronizer", "text": "One-way synchronisation from a source folder to a target: mirror = false copies files that are new or changed (size or a newer date, compared to the millisecond; a source newer by less than 2 s is ignored when the target's time is an exact even second, as on FAT32 drives); never deletes mirror = true the same, and deletes from the target what no longer exists at the source SyncResult lists what was copied, updated, deleted and what failed (files in use or without permission are skipped; the rest continues). For progress, call plan(source, target) to know the bytes and files to copy, then sync(", "url": "en/docs/backup-sync.html#sync-directorysynchronizer"}, {"title": "Watching folders", "heading": "", "text": "try (WatchOperation w = OctopusFile.watch(\"C:/Users/ana/Downloads\") .filterExtension(\"pdf\") .onCreate(e -> System.out.println(\"new: \" + e.path())) .onDelete(e -> System.out.println(\"gone: \" + e.path())) .start()) { // ... the watcher runs in the background until close() } Callbacks: onCreate , onModify , onDelete , onEvent (all), onOverflow (too many events at once — re-read the folder). Filters: filterExtension(\"pdf\") or filter(path -> …) . Each FileEvent has path() , kind() and timestamp() . Built on the JDK WatchService . The facade watches one folder (not its subfolders); to watch the subf", "url": "en/docs/watching.html"}, {"title": "Storage", "heading": "External devices", "text": "for (Path p : ExternalDevices.listRemovable()) { // pen drives, memory cards, USB disks System.out.println(ExternalDevices.volumeLabel(p) + \" (\" + ExternalDevices.fileSystemType(p) + \")\"); } isRemovable(root) checks one drive with the volume:isRemovable attribute of its file store (CD/DVD drives are left out): reliable on Windows. On Linux and macOS use listRemovable() , which looks at the mounts under /media/<user> , /run/media/<user> and /Volumes (except Macintosh HD ).", "url": "en/docs/storage.html#external-devices"}, {"title": "Storage", "heading": "Synced cloud folders", "text": "for (CloudSyncFolders.CloudFolder c : CloudSyncFolders.detect()) { System.out.println(c.name() + \" -> \" + c.path()); } Finds the folders kept in sync by the official apps: OneDrive, Dropbox, Google Drive, iCloud, MEGA, Nextcloud, ownCloud, pCloud, Box and Yandex Disk. They are plain local folders.", "url": "en/docs/storage.html#synced-cloud-folders"}, {"title": "Storage", "heading": "WebDAV clouds", "text": "Nextcloud, ownCloud, Box, pCloud, Yandex Disk, Koofr, NAS boxes and any WebDAV server, with java.net.http . WebDavStorage dav = new WebDavStorage(\"https://cloud.example.com/remote.php/dav/files/ana/\", \"ana\", \"app-password\".toCharArray()); dav.testConnection(); for (WebDavStorage.Entry e : dav.list(\"/Documents\")) { System.out.println(e.name() + (e.directory() ? \"/\" : \"\")); } dav.upload(Path.of(\"report.pdf\"), \"/Documents/report.pdf\", bytes -> { }, () -> false); Also mkdir , delete , move , download . Errors are clear ( wrong user name or password , not found , the cloud storage is full ). The XM", "url": "en/docs/storage.html#webdav-clouds"}, {"title": "Storage", "heading": "Network shares (SMB)", "text": "NetworkShares.Address a = NetworkShares.parse(\"\\\\\\\\nas\\\\photos\"); // also //nas/photos, smb://ana@nas/photos Path local = NetworkShares.connect(a, \"ana\", \"secret\".toCharArray()); The operating system makes the connection and OctopusFile uses the local path it produces: a UNC path on Windows (connected with the Windows API; with no user, the current session is used), gio mount (GVfs) on Linux, mount volume on macOS. The password never goes on a command line. connect blocks for up to about 40 s; call it outside the UI thread. isAvailable(address) checks without connecting.", "url": "en/docs/storage.html#network-shares-smb"}, {"title": "Storage", "heading": "One interface for all — StorageProvider", "text": "LocalDiskStorage , ExternalDevices , NetworkShareStorage , WebDavStorage and InMemoryBucketStorage (for tests) implement StorageProvider : getName() , isAvailable() , openRead(path) , openWrite(path) , delete(path) , exists(path) . StorageRegistry keeps providers by key, so your own (FTP, S3, …) can be plugged in: StorageRegistry registry = new StorageRegistry(); registry.register(\"nas\", new NetworkShareStorage(\"nas\", \"photos\")); try (InputStream in = registry.openRead(\"nas\", \"2026/beach.jpg\")) { ... }", "url": "en/docs/storage.html#one-interface-for-all-storageprovider"}, {"title": "Vault & secrets", "heading": "Vault — an encrypted folder", "text": "try (Vault vault = Vault.create(root, \"a long password\".toCharArray())) { vault.add(contract, \"Contracts/contract.pdf\", null); } try (Vault vault = Vault.unlock(root, \"a long password\".toCharArray())) { for (Vault.Entry e : vault.list()) { vault.extract(e, out.resolve(e.name()), null); } } catch (Vault.WrongPasswordException ex) { System.out.println(\"wrong password\"); } Cipher AES-256-GCM, files in 1 MiB chunks, each with its own nonce and tag Integrity chunk number and a \"last chunk\" flag are authenticated: reordering, removing, cutting or changing a chunk is detected and the extraction fails", "url": "en/docs/vault.html#vault-an-encrypted-folder"}, {"title": "Vault & secrets", "heading": "CredentialVault — secrets at rest", "text": "Keeps passwords and tokens encrypted with a random key stored in a private file (owner-only permissions on POSIX systems, set when the file is created). A damaged key file is reported with an exception and never replaced : a new key would make every saved secret unreadable. Use it for things your application must send again later (for example a WebDAV app password). CredentialVault secrets = new CredentialVault(dir.resolve(\"keys\")); String sealed = secrets.seal(\"app password\".toCharArray()); // safe to store char[] back = secrets.open(sealed);", "url": "en/docs/vault.html#credentialvault-secrets-at-rest"}, {"title": "Vault & secrets", "heading": "EncryptionEngine — one file, one password", "text": "OctopusFile.of().encrypt(source, target, password) / decrypt(...) : AES-256-GCM with a PBKDF2 key (210 000 iterations). Since 1.3 the format is in 1 MiB blocks ( \"OFE2\" , salt, then [length][nonce][data + tag] per block, each block authenticated with its number and a \"last block\" flag), so memory use stays small for big files and a cut file is detected. Files of the old format ( [salt][iv][ciphertext + tag] ) are still decrypted. The target appears only when everything was verified: a wrong password leaves no partial file and does not touch an existing target. The cryptographic design has been", "url": "en/docs/vault.html#encryptionengine-one-file-one-password"}, {"title": "Sharing nearby", "heading": "", "text": "Send files and folders to another computer on the same network — no internet, no cloud, no external library. // receiving computer try (LanShare receiver = new LanShare(\"Ana's laptop\", in -> { System.out.println(in.sender() + \" sends \" + in.names() + \" - code \" + in.code()); new Thread(() -> { try { in.accept(inbox, null, () -> false); // or in.decline() } catch (IOException ex) { ex.printStackTrace(); } }).start(); }, null)) { // null = all network interfaces // ... } // sending computer try (LanShare lan = new LanShare(\"Office PC\", null, null)) { // no handler: only looks for computers LanSh", "url": "en/docs/lan-share.html"}, {"title": "Sharing nearby", "heading": "How it works", "text": "Discovery computers that accept files announce themselves by UDP broadcast on port 47821 every 3 s; peers() lists those seen in the last 12 s Key exchange X25519 per connection (a new key every time); the sender first sends a hash of its key (commitment), receives the key of the receiver, and only then reveals its own Encryption AES-256-GCM per 64 KB frame, nonce = direction + counter (never reused) Confirmation both sides get the same 6-digit code derived from the shared secret: if the codes match, nobody is in the middle. Thanks to the commitment, a computer in the middle cannot search for k", "url": "en/docs/lan-share.html#how-it-works"}, {"title": "Sharing nearby", "heading": "Folder sync (since 2.1)", "text": "LanShare.sync makes a folder on the other computer like a folder of this one, sending only the files that are new or changed there. The receiver turns sync on with the four-argument constructor: // receiving computer: ordinary sends and folder syncs try (LanShare receiver = new LanShare(\"Ana's laptop\", in -> { if (in.isSync()) { System.out.println(in.sender() + \" syncs \\\"\" + in.folderName() + \"\\\"\" + (in.mirror() ? \" (mirror)\" : \"\") + \" - code \" + in.code()); new Thread(() -> { try { LanShare.SyncReport r = in.acceptSync(backups.resolve(in.folderName()), null, () -> false); System.out.println(r", "url": "en/docs/lan-share.html#folder-sync-since-21"}, {"title": "Security", "heading": "", "text": "What OctopusFile protects, what the 1.3 review changed, and the limits you should know before relying on it. Every point below has a test in selftest/.../SecurityTest.java or EdgeCaseTest.java .", "url": "en/docs/security.html"}, {"title": "Security", "heading": "What is protected", "text": "[Vault](vault.md) AES-256-GCM in 1 MiB blocks, PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 (310 000 iterations), encrypted index of names; reordering, cutting or changing a block is detected [EncryptionEngine](vault.md) the same block design for a single file, with a password (PBKDF2 with 210 000 iterations) [CredentialVault](vault.md) small secrets encrypted with a random key in an owner-only file [Sharing nearby](lan-share.md) X25519 + AES-256-GCM, 6-digit confirmation code, consent of the receiver for every transfer [ZIP extraction](compression.md) entries that would land outside the destination (\"zip slip\") are re", "url": "en/docs/security.html#what-is-protected"}, {"title": "Security", "heading": "Changes in 1.3", "text": "Sharing nearby Commitment before the key exchange. Before 1.3 both computers sent their public keys at the same time, so a computer in the middle could try millions of its own keys in a few seconds until the two 6-digit codes matched — the code gave almost no protection against an active attacker. Now the sender first sends a hash of its key, receives the key of the receiver, and only then reveals its own; a key that does not match the hash ends the connection. An attacker in the middle has one chance in a million. This is protocol version 2; computers with version 1 are refused with a clear m", "url": "en/docs/security.html#changes-in-13"}, {"title": "Security", "heading": "Limits", "text": "Not independently audited. The design follows common practice and is tested, but nobody outside the project has reviewed it. Do not rely on it alone for high-value data. Vault: an attacker who can write to the vault folder can put back an older index.bin (rollback) or delete files; they cannot read or change contents without the password. Names, sizes and dates are encrypted, the number and the approximate sizes of the files are not. Passwords need at least 6 characters: ask for more in your UI. CredentialVault: the key sits next to the data, protected by the permissions of the user account. I", "url": "en/docs/security.html#limits"}, {"title": "Security", "heading": "Reporting a problem", "text": "Open an issue on the repository without the details of the vulnerability and ask for a private contact, or write to the author directly.", "url": "en/docs/security.html#reporting-a-problem"}, {"title": "2.0 LTS", "heading": "", "text": "OctopusFile 2.0 is the official long-term support (LTS) version . 2.0.0 was released on 21 November 2026 ; it is supported until 21 November 2028 . The current version is shown at the bottom of every page of this website.", "url": "en/docs/lts.html"}, {"title": "2.0 LTS", "heading": "The LTS policy", "text": "Support 2 years from the release of 2.0.0: 2.0.x versions with bug and security fixes only — no new features, no API changes API no breaking change in the whole 2.x line; anything deprecated in 2.x is removed only in 3.0. A test ( ApiSignatureTest ) compares every public type, constructor, method and field with the frozen list api/octopusfile-api.txt , so a change cannot slip into a 2.0.x by accident Java Java 17 and newer; tested on Windows, Linux and macOS by the CI Formats everything written by 2.0 (vault, encrypted files, snapshot repositories, saved index) stays readable by every later ve", "url": "en/docs/lts.html#the-lts-policy"}, {"title": "2.0 LTS", "heading": "Phases", "text": "2.0.0-alpha API cleanup: removed classes, names, internal types out of the public API alpha.1 done 2.0.0-beta Transfer (progress, cancel) for every long operation; snapshot repositories on WebDAV; one rule for errors beta.1 done 2.0.0-rc API frozen; tests of every file format with files written by 1.x; complete Javadoc; final migration guide rc.1 done 2.0.0 release; start of the 2 years of support released 2026-11-21", "url": "en/docs/lts.html#phases"}, {"title": "2.0 LTS", "heading": "2.0.x versions", "text": "Fix versions of 2.0, built on the lts/2.0 branch. Each one only fixes; the API stays exactly the same. 2.0.4 2026-11-21 The last two findings of the audit: copying a folder onto an existing folder no longer replaces files without being asked (it refuses before copying anything), and GlobalConfiguration.setIsOverwriteByDefault(true) now really makes copies and moves replace 2.0.3 2026-11-21 65 fixes from a full audit with a reproduction for every bug, before 2.0 goes into FileExp: a new rule — a walk never goes into a link or NTFS junction (recursive deletes used to delete what a junction point", "url": "en/docs/lts.html#20x-versions"}, {"title": "2.0 LTS", "heading": "2.1 — new features on main", "text": "2.1 is the first minor version of the 2.x line. It only adds : no public type, method, field or constructor was removed or changed ( ApiSignatureTest shows only added lines), and everything written by 2.0 stays readable. It is built on main ; lts/2.0 keeps the 2.0.x fixes only. 2.1.0 2026-11-22 [Versions of one file](backup-sync.md) in a snapshot repository and restoring one of them; [pause and resume](transfers.md) of transfers ( Transfer.State.PAUSED ); backup(…).toDefault() uses the [default backup folder](backup-sync.md); [folder sync](lan-share.md) between two computers on the local netwo", "url": "en/docs/lts.html#21-new-features-on-main"}, {"title": "2.0 LTS", "heading": "Migrating from 1.x", "text": "Code that compiled on 1.4 without removal warnings needs only the renames below. To find the uses, compile against 1.4 with -Xlint:removal . Removed (deprecated since 1.4): FileManager , OctopusFile.getFileManager() the OctopusFile facade ( copy , move , delete , write ) or java.nio.file.Files ; rename(a, b) → move(a).to(b) . Note: FileManager.move / rename replaced an existing target, move(a).to(b) refuses it (use Files.move(a, b, REPLACE_EXISTING) to keep the old behaviour) DirectoryManager , getDirectoryManager() OctopusFile.list(...) , createDirectory(...) or Files ; deleteDirectoryRecursi", "url": "en/docs/lts.html#migrating-from-1x"}, {"title": "2.0 LTS", "heading": "Checklist", "text": "Compile your code against 1.4.1 with -Xlint:removal and replace every use it reports (tables above). Switch to Java 17 or newer and to the 2.0 JAR. Rename FileReader , BufferedReader , FileWriter , BufferedWriter of com.octopusfile (imports only). Where the compiler asks, catch or declare IOException around the module classes; keep catching OctopusFileException around the OctopusFile facade. Nothing to do for your data: vaults, encrypted files, snapshot repositories, saved indexes, saved secrets and the trash written by 1.x open as they are (see below).", "url": "en/docs/lts.html#checklist"}, {"title": "2.0 LTS", "heading": "API freeze", "text": "From 2.0.0-rc.1 the public API is frozen. Every public type, method and field of the exported packages is listed in api/octopusfile-api.txt , and the test suite fails if the code differs from it. Until 2.0.0 only fixes go in; during the 2 years of support, 2.x versions may only add to the API, never remove or change it. Every public element has Javadoc.", "url": "en/docs/lts.html#api-freeze"}, {"title": "2.0 LTS", "heading": "Formats you can rely on", "text": "Vault ( FXV1 files, vault.properties version 1) 1.0 yes EncryptionEngine format 2 ( OFE2 ) and format 1 1.3 / 1.0 yes Snapshot repository format 1 1.4 yes Saved index format 1 1.4 yes; a file of another format gives an IOException (build the index again) Sharing nearby protocol 2 1.3 yes Saved secrets ( CredentialVault ) 1.x yes Trash folder 1.x yes This is tested, not only promised: selftest/fixtures holds files written by the real 1.2.0 and 1.4.1 JARs (encrypted files of formats 1 and 2, vaults, saved secrets, trash, plain and encrypted snapshot repositories, a saved index), and FormatCompat", "url": "en/docs/lts.html#formats-you-can-rely-on"}, {"title": "module-info", "heading": "", "text": "The JAR is the module com.octopusfile . Packages marked @Internal are not exported. / OctopusFile: a file toolkit for Java 17+ with no external libraries. <p> Packages marked {@code @Internal} (support. , infrastructure.concurrency, .filesystem, .resources) are not exported: other modules cannot use them. On the classpath (non-modular applications) the JAR works as before. <p> X25519 (sharing nearby) comes from the {@code jdk.crypto.ec} provider; in a custom runtime add it with {@code jlink --add-modules jdk.crypto.ec}. / module com.octopusfile { requires java.logging; requires java.net.http; ", "url": "en/docs/module-info.html"}];
